  "issue_number": 78,
  "issue_title": "[bug] Math grader marks LaTeX-grouped thousands (\\boxed{2{,}048}) wrong",
  "issue_body": "**Describe the bug**\n\nThe math grader (`src/trinity/orchestration/reward.py`) strips *bare* digit-grouping commas (`1,000` \u2192 `1000`), but not the LaTeX `{,}` grouping form that MATH-500 answers commonly use. `{,}` renders as a comma, so `\\boxed{2{,}048}` is the same value as `2048` \u2014 but it grades **wrong**.\n\n**Expected vs actual**\n- Expected: `\\boxed{2{,}048}` grades equal to `2048`.\n- Actual: score `0.0` (false negative). Same for `\\boxed{1{,}000}`, `\\boxed{1{,}234{,}567}`, `The answer is 1{,}000`, etc.\n\n**Repro**\n\n```python\nfrom trinity.orchestration.reward import score_text\nprint(score_text(\"math500\", r\"\\boxed{2{,}048}\", \"2048\"))  # 0.0  (should be 1.0)\nprint(score_text(\"math500\", r\"\\boxed{1{,}000}\", \"1000\"))  # 0.0  (should be 1.0)\n```\n\n**Root cause**\n\nThe braces defeat both halves of the grader:\n- `extract_last_number(\"...1{,}000\")` splits it into `1` and `000` (returns `000`) because the thousands regex expects a *bare* comma.\n- `normalize_math_answer` only strips a bare comma between digits, so the braced form survives and never equals the plain reference.\n\n**Suggested fix**\n\nNormalize `{,}` \u2192 `,` early in both `extract_last_number` and `normalize_math_answer`, so the existing (already-tested) comma handling removes it. Additive to the bare-comma logic; a comma-separated list (`1,2,3`) and genuinely wrong answers must stay wrong (no false positives).\n\n**Environment**\n- Python 3.12; commit `cf9da4d` (current `main`).",
  "merged_at": "2026-07-10T14:33:02.000Z",
